<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Comparing consistency using standard deviation</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>{@nm1@} and {@nm2@} ran 400 m six times each at a training camp in Iten. Their times, in seconds, were</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Runner</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 1</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 2</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 3</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 4</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 5</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Race 6</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@nm1@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t1d[6]@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@nm2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@t2d[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>(a) Find the standard deviation of {@nm1@}'s times, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div>\(\sigma =\) [[input:ans1]] s [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the standard deviation of {@nm2@}'s times, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div>\(\sigma =\) [[input:ans2]] s [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Who is the more consistent runner?</p><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Which reason supports your answer to (c)?</p><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The variance is the mean of the squared deviations from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root, in seconds. Divide by \(n = 6\).</p><p>(a) {@nm1@}: \(\bar{x} \approx {@m1d@}\), \(\Sigma(x - \bar{x})^2 \approx {@dsp1@}\), so \(\sigma^2 \approx {@vr1d@}\) and \(\sigma \approx {@sd1d@}\) s.</p><p>(b) {@nm2@}: \(\bar{x} \approx {@m2d@}\), \(\Sigma(x - \bar{x})^2 \approx {@dsp2@}\), so \(\sigma^2 \approx {@vr2d@}\) and \(\sigma \approx {@sd2d@}\) s.</p><p>(c) {@cnm@} has the smaller standard deviation, so {@cnm@} is the more consistent runner.</p><p>(d) A smaller standard deviation means the times stay closer to the runner's own mean. Consistency is about spread, not about the mean: [[if test="is(cs=1)"]]here the more consistent runner is actually slower on average.[[else]]here the more consistent runner also happens to be faster on average, but that is not why the times are consistent.[[/if]]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
